"Checkmate: Breaking the Memory Wall with Optimal Tensor Rematerialization (https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.02653)\nciteturn9academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.1 years ago; Title: Checkmate: Breaking the Memory Wall with Optimal Tensor Rematerialization ... We formalize the problem of trading-off DNN training time and memory requirements as the tensor rematerialization optimization problem, a generalization of prior checkpointing strategies.\nTitle: Checkmate: Breaking the Memory Wall with Optimal Tensor Rematerialization\nAuthors: Paras Jain, Ajay Jain, Aniruddha Nrusimha, Amir Gholami, Pieter Abbeel, Kurt Keutzer, Ion Stoica, Joseph E. Gonzalez\nDate: Mon Oct  7 07:54:06 2019\n\nWe formalize the problem of trading-off DNN training time and memory requirements as the tensor rematerialization optimization problem, a generalization of prior checkpointing strategies. We introduce Checkmate, a system that solves for optimal rematerialization schedules in reasonable times (under an hour) using off-the-shelf MILP solvers or near-optimal schedules with an approximation algorithm, then uses these schedules to accelerate millions of training iterations. Our method scales to complex, realistic architectures and is hardware-aware through the use of accelerator-specific, profile-based cost models. In addition to reducing training cost, Checkmate enables real-world networks to be trained with up to 5.1x larger input sizes. Checkmate is an open-source project, available at https://github.com/parasj/checkmate.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIssues · ingonyama-zk/icicle · GitHub (https://github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle/issues)\nciteturn9search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; #1047 In ingonyama-zk/icicle; ... #1046 In ingonyama-zk/icicle;\n\ningonyama-zk / icicle Public\n\n  * Notifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\n  * Fork 171\n  * Star\n\n  * Code\n  * Issues 27\n  * Pull requests 22\n  * Actions\n  * Projects\n  * Security and quality 0\n  * Insights\n\n# All issues\n\nNew issue Issue creation is restricted in this repository\n\n## Issues\n\nSearch Issues\n\nis:issue state:open\n\nis:issue state:open\n\n[Input: Search Issues]\n\nSearch\n\n## Search results\n\n  * ### [FEAT]: Goldilocks/G64 STARK-LDE NTT: benchmark contract informed by Qingming layout-fusion results\n\ntype:featureNew feature or requestNew feature or request\n\nStatus: Open.\n\n#1047 In ingonyama-zk/icicle;\n\n· uulong950 opened on Jul 2, 2026\n\n  * ### NTT digit-reversal (reorder_digits_and_normalize) is memory-bound on Blackwell — ~2× recoverable via coalesced permutation\n\nStatus: Open.\n\n#1046 In ingonyama-zk/icicle;\n\n· pscamillo opened on May 29, 2026\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCheckmate: Breaking the Memory Wall with Optimal Tensor Rematerialization (https://proceedings.mlsys.org/paper_files/paper/2020/file/0b816ae8f06f8dd3543dc3d9ef196cab-Paper.pdf)\nciteturn9search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.7 years ago; Checkmate: Breaking the Memory Wall with Optimal Tensor Rematerialization ... We formalize the problem of trading-off DNN training time and memory requirements as the tensor rematerialization optimization problem, a generalization of prior checkpointing strategies. ... Checkmate is an open-source project, available at https://github.com/parasj/checkmate. ... Proceedings of the 3rd MLSys Conference, Austin, TX, USA, 2020.\nCheckmate: Breaking the Memory Wall with Optimal Tensor Rematerialization\n\nParas Jain* 1, Ajay Jain* 1, Aniruddha Nrusimha 1, Amir Gholami 1, Pieter Abbeel 1, Kurt Keutzer 1, Ion Stoica 1, Joseph E. Gonzalez 1\n\nABSTRACT  \nWe formalize the problem of trading-off DNN training time and memory requirements as the tensor rematerialization optimization problem, a generalization of prior checkpointing strategies. We introduce Checkmate, a system that solves for optimal rematerialization schedules in reasonable times (under an hour) using off-the-shelf MILP solvers or near-optimal schedules with an approximation algorithm, then uses these schedules to accelerate millions of training iterations. Our method scales to complex, realistic architectures and is hardware-aware through the use of accelerator-specific, profile-based cost models. In addition to reducing training cost, Checkmate enables real-world networks to be trained with up to 5.1× larger input sizes. Checkmate is an open-source project, available at https://github.com/parasj/checkmate.\n\n1 INTRODUCTION  \nDeep learning training workloads demand large amounts of high bandwidth memory. Researchers are pushing the memory capacity limits of hardware accelerators such as GPUs by training neural networks on high-resolution images (Dong et al., 2016; Kim et al., 2016; Tai et al., 2017), 3D point-clouds (Chen et al., 2017; Yang et al., 2018), and long natural language sequences (Vaswani et al., 2017; Devlin et al., 2018; Child et al., 2019). In these applications, training memory usage is dominated by the intermediate activation tensors needed for backpropagation (Figure 3).\n\nThe limited availability of high bandwidth on-device memory creates a memory wall that stifles exploration of novel architectures. Across applications, authors of state-of-the-art models cite memory as a limiting factor in deep neural network (DNN) design (Krizhevsky et al., 2012; He et al., 2016; Chen et al., 2016a; Gomez et al., 2017; Pohlen et al., 2017; Child et al., 2019; Liu et al., 2019; Dai et al., 2019).\n\nAs there is insufficient RAM to cache all activation tensors for backpropagation, some select tensors can be discarded during forward evaluation. When a discarded tensor is necessary as a dependency for gradient calculation, the tensor can be rematerialized. As illustrated in Figure 1, rematerializing values allows a large DNN to fit within memory at the expense of additional computation.\n\n*Equal contribution  1Department of EECS, UC Berkeley.  \nCorrespondence to: Paras Jain <parasj@berkeley.edu>.\n\nProceedings of the 3rd MLSys Conference, Austin, TX, USA, 2020. Copyright 2020 by the author(s).\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nFigure 1 line chart comparing memory over time for two strategies. Y-axis: RAM used (GB), with ticks 0, 10, 20, 30. X-axis: Time. Red line labeled “Retain all activations” rises to about 29 GB and stays flat. Blue line labeled “Rematerialize activations” stays much lower with repeated spikes and shaded blue rematerialized regions. Two vertical dotted markers appear near the right side, one red and one blue.\n</visual_element>\n\nFigure 1. This 32-layer deep neural network requires 30GB of memory during training in order to cache forward pass activations for the backward pass. Freeing certain activations early and rematerializing them later reduces memory requirements by 21GB at the cost of a modest runtime increase. Rematerialized layers are denoted as shaded blue regions. We present Checkmate, a system to rematerialize large neural networks optimally. Checkmate is hardware-aware, memory-aware and supports arbitrary DAGs.\n\nGriewank & Walther (2000) and Chen et al. (2016b) present heuristics for rematerialization when the forward pass forms a linear graph, or path graph. They refer to the problem as checkpointing. However, their approaches cannot be applied generally to nonlinear DNN structures such as residual connections, and rely on the strong assumption that all nodes in the graph have the same cost. Prior work also assumes that gradients may never be rematerialized. These assumptions limit the efficiency and generality of prior approaches.\n\nOur work formalizes tensor rematerialization as a constrained optimization problem. Using off-the-shelf numerical solvers, we are able to discover optimal rematerializa---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[FEAT]: Goldilocks/G64 STARK-LDE NTT: benchmark contract informed by Qingming layout-fusion results · Issue #1047 · ingonyama-zk/icicle · GitHub (https://github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle/issues/1047)\nciteturn9search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Please consider adding or discussing a Goldilocks/G64 STARK-LDE NTT benchmark contract in ICICLE, using Qingming G64 NTT as a reference implementation for the benchmark methodology and layout-fusion design. ... In issue #1046, the BabyBear Blackwell profile shows that `reorder_digits_and_normalize` can dominate the forward NTT once butterfly kernels are already close to the memory ceiling. ... Reference implementation: `https://github.com/uulong950/qingming-g64-ntt`\n\n## Motivation\n\nIn issue #1046, the BabyBear Blackwell profile shows that `reorder_digits_and_normalize` can dominate the forward NTT once butterfly kernels are already close to the memory ceiling. The report shows the reorder pass taking a large share of NTT kernel time, with low global-store efficiency caused by scatter-style digit reversal.\n\nThat observation matches the design lesson from Qingming G64 NTT: high-performance NTT is not only a field-arithmetic problem. For STARK-LDE workloads, end-to-end performance is often determined by whether the layout contract, radix decomposition, transpose strategy, twiddle application, and reorder/prelayout stages are designed together.\n\nQingming’s advantage is that it keeps the native Goldilocks/STARK-LDE boundary intact instead of benchmarking a proxy problem. It uses the exact Goldilocks modulus, exact 8x LDE expansion, and a large `2^27` transform domain. It also reports fast-interface, standard-interface, and prelayout costs separately, with median and p95 statistics, and requires correctness gates before treating a performance number as valid.\n\nA similar benchmark contract in ICICLE would make it easier to evaluate Goldilocks/STARK-oriented NTT performance across backends and avoid misleading comparisons where one implementation includes layout or reorder costs and another hides them.\n\nThis could also provide a useful framework for future ICICLE work on coalesced permutation, fused transpose/twiddle stages, and layout-aware NTT design.\n\n## Reference implementation / AMD HIP environment\n\nReference implementation: `https://github.com/uulong950/qingming-g64-ntt`\n\nValidated environment:\n\n  * GPU: AMD Radeon RX 7900 XTX\n  * Backend: HIP / ROCm\n  * ROCm: `7.2.4`\n  * HIP: `7.2.53211`\n  * Workload: native Goldilocks/G64 STARK-LDE NTT\n  * Modulus: `0xFFFFFFFF00000001`\n  * Logical size: `2^24`\n  * LDE expansion: exact 8x\n  * Transform domain: `2^27`\n  * Batch size: 1\n  * Primary benchmark: device-resident forward NTT\n\nReactions are currently unavailable--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrequently Asked Questions | ICICLE Docs (https://dev.ingonyama.com/start/integration-%26-support/faq_and_troubleshooting)\nciteturn9search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 1.2 years ago;   * The core CUDA backend is source available under Ingonyama’s custom ICICLE-SA license. ... GitHub issues on the ICICLE repo are the primary support channel.\n\nHow much speedup can I expect using ICICLE?▾\n\nCheck out the Benchmark Dashboard to explore real-world results.\n\nWho is using ICICLE today?▾\n\nICICLE is used by leading projects and research teams across cryptography and infrastructure. See the full list here.\n\nWhere can I get help if I encounter issues?▾\n\nGitHub issues on the ICICLE repo are the primary support channel. Community discussions often happen in our Discord Server’s ICICLE channel.\n\nCan I contribute to ICICLE?▾\n\nYes — contributions to ICICLE are welcome! You can submit pull requests or open issues on our public GitHub repository. If you're working on research or development aligned with ICICLE, consider applying to our Ingonyama Research Grant Program 2025.\n\nWhat is the maximum MSM size tested?▾\n\nMSM sizes up to 2^{30} have been tested successfully on the 3090Ti. Note that this applies only to certain backends. For example, Metal may not yet support streaming, which could limit maximum MSM size on that backend.\n\nWhat class of GPU is required to run ICICLE?▾\n\nNvidia CUDA GPUs only. Some mobile/workstation GPUs (e.g. Quadro P520) may hit compilation issues that require patching CMake flags.\n\nWhy does my benchmark report zero time per iteration?▾\n\nLikely due to LLVM optimization eliminating empty loops. Use `black_box` to prevent optimization during benchmarks.\n\nIs omega equal to root_of_unity?▾\n\nYes, but ICICLE stores precomputed omegas for multiple powers. The specific omega used depends on the NTT size.\n\nIs there documentation for ntt_template_kernel_shared_rev?▾\n\nNo formal docs. But the butterfly operation is here: ntt.cuh L153.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCheckmate: Breaking the Memory Wall with Optimal Tensor Rematerialization | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/1910.02653)\nciteturn9search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.5 years ago; Crawled: last month; # Checkmate: Breaking the Memory Wall with Optimal Tensor Rematerialization ... @misc{jain2020checkmatebreakingmemorywall, title={Checkmate: Breaking the Memory Wall with Optimal Tensor Rematerialization}, author={Paras Jain and Ajay Jain and Aniruddha Nrusimha and Amir Gholami and Pieter Abbeel and Kurt Keutzer and Ion Stoica and Joseph E.\nSubmitted 14 May 2020\n\n# Checkmate: Breaking the Memory Wall with Optimal Tensor Rematerialization\n\nParas JainAjay Jain\n\nAN\n\nAniruddha NrusimhaAmir GholamiPieter AbbeelKurt KeutzerIon StoicaJoseph E. Gonzalez\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe formalize the problem of trading-off DNN training time and memory requirements as the tensor rematerialization optimization problem, a generalization of prior checkpointing strategies. We introduce Checkmate, a system that solves for optimal rematerialization schedules in reasonable times (under 